# PHP / Laravel security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to PHP/Laravel-
specific, OWASP-relevant risks and the framework API to use instead.
Applies only to `*.php` files.

## Mass assignment

- Every Eloquent model that is ever constructed from request data
  declares `$fillable` (an explicit allowlist) or `$guarded`; an empty
  `$guarded = []` mass-assigns every column, attacker-controlled fields
  (`is_admin`, `role`, `user_id` on someone else's record) included.
- Build a create/update from `$request->validated()` (the Form Request's
  validated subset), not raw `$request->all()`/`$request->input()` — even
  with `$fillable` set, validating first catches type/shape problems
  `$fillable` alone does not.

## SQL and data access

- Use Eloquent/the query builder's parameter binding
  (`where('email', $email)`, `DB::select('... where id = ?', [$id])`) for
  every value that varies with input; never interpolate a variable
  directly into a raw SQL string (`DB::select("... where id = $id")`,
  `DB::statement("... = '{$value}'")`) — that is SQL injection regardless
  of how "internal" the input looks.
- `DB::raw()`/`whereRaw()`/`selectRaw()` still take bindings as a second
  argument for any variable part of the expression — pass the value
  there, not by interpolating it into the raw string.

## Output encoding (Blade/XSS)

- Blade's `{{ $value }}` escapes through `htmlspecialchars` by default;
  use it for anything that can contain user-influenced content.
- `{!! $value !!}` performs no escaping — reserve it for content the
  application itself fully controls and trusts (a sanitized rich-text
  field explicitly passed through a purifier, not raw user input) and
  say so at the point of use when it is used at all.

## CSRF and state-changing requests

- Every state-changing route (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE) reached from a
  browser session relies on Laravel's `VerifyCsrfToken`/
  `ValidateCsrfToken` middleware (the default for the `web` middleware
  group) and includes `@csrf` in the form or the `X-CSRF-TOKEN`/
  `X-XSRF-TOKEN` header for an AJAX call.
- Adding a route to the CSRF-exempt list is a deliberate, narrow decision
  for a genuinely stateless endpoint (a signed webhook, an API token
  route already outside the `web` group) — never a workaround for a
  request that is failing CSRF verification for an unrelated reason.

## Secrets and configuration

- Read secrets from `.env`/`config/*.php` via the `config()` helper; never
  commit a real credential, API key, or signing secret into a versioned
  file (`.env.example` gets placeholder values only).
- Laravel's `APP_KEY` (used for encryption and signed URLs) is generated
  per environment (`php artisan key:generate`) and never shared between
  environments or committed to version control.

## Queues and background work

- A queue job handling anything security- or money-relevant is written
  assuming at-least-once delivery: guard the side effect with
  `ShouldBeUnique`, a `uniqueId()`, or an idempotency check recorded
  before the effect runs, not an assumption that retries "shouldn't
  normally happen."

## Dependency hygiene

- Run `composer audit` when introducing or updating dependencies, or when
  asked to check for known vulnerabilities in the project's Composer
  packages.
